     "t": "cover",
     "kicker": "Bijoya Dashami · Wed 21 Oct 2026",
     "title": "Bijoya Dashami: how Bengal says goodbye",
     "bn": "বিজয়া দশমী",
     "sub": "Darpan bisarjan, boron, sindoor khela, bisarjan",
     "photo": {
      "src": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/b2/Sovabazar_Royal_Durga_Idol_Immersion_Arrangement_-_River_Hooghly_-_Baghbazar_Ghat_-_Kolkata_2014-10-03_9208.jpg",
      "artist": "Biswarup Ganguly",
      "license": "CC BY 3.0",
      "cx": 0.5,
      "cy": 0.45
     }
    },
    {
     "t": "step",
     "kicker": "Morning",
     "num": 1,
     "title": "Darpan bisarjan",
     "bn": "দর্পণ বিসর্জন",
     "body": "After a short Dashami puja the priest holds a mirror so the goddess is reflected in a bowl of water, and immerses her reflection. She has now left the clay image. Then shanti jal is sprinkled."
    },
    {
     "t": "step",
     "kicker": "The farewell",
     "num": 2,
     "title": "Boron",
     "bn": "বরণ",
     "body": "Married women circle a boron dala, a plate with a lamp, sindoor, betel leaves and sweets, before the goddess. They touch sweets to her lips and wipe her face with a betel leaf, as if drying a daughter's tears."
    },
    {
     "t": "step",
     "kicker": "Then",
     "num": 3,
     "title": "Sindoor khela",
     "bn": "সিঁদুর খেলা",
     "body": "Women smear sindoor on each other's foreheads, cheeks and conch-shell bangles, wishing each other a long, happy married life. Traditionally married women; some pujas now welcome more. Ask the organisers."
    },
    {
     "t": "photo",
     "photo": {
      "src": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/68/Bijoya_Dashami.jpg",
      "artist": "Diganta Talukdar",
      "license": "CC BY 4.0",
      "cx": 0.5,
      "cy": 0.45
     },
     "line": "Wear something you don't mind staining: sindoor stains fabric"
    },
    {
     "t": "step",
     "kicker": "Afternoon to evening",
     "num": 4,
     "title": "Bisarjan",
     "bn": "বিসর্জন",
     "body": "The idols go in procession to the Hooghly with dhak and cries of Asche bochor abar hobe, it will happen again next year. Kolkata spreads immersions over Dashami and the days after, on an official schedule."
    },
    {
     "t": "step",
     "kicker": "After the immersion",
     "num": 5,
     "title": "Shubho Bijoya",
     "bn": "শুভ বিজয়া",
     "body": "Younger people touch elders' feet (pranam), people of similar age embrace (kolakuli), and everyone shares sweets like naru, sandesh and nimki. The visits go on for days."
    },
    {
     "t": "end",
     "title": "Asche bochor abar hobe",
     "bn": "আসছে বছর আবার হবে",
     "ask": "Send it to someone far from home"
    }
   ],
   "caption": {
    "en": "Bijoya Dashami 2026 in Kolkata: the last day of Durga Puja, rite by rite 🙏\n\nDashami is Wednesday 21 October. The priest immerses the goddess's reflection in a mirror, darpan bisarjan, and from that moment she has left the idol.\n\nMarried women perform boron, wiping her face with a betel leaf as if drying a daughter's tears, then play sindoor khela with each other. The idols go to the Hooghly in procession, and Kolkata's immersions run over Dashami and the days after on a schedule set by the authorities.\n\nAt the ghats, follow police instructions and keep small children away from the riverbank at peak times.\n\nWho gets your first Shubho Bijoya call? Tell us in the comments 👇",
